# Defanged reconstruction of a real malicious package, for scanner tests.
# Never install or run. See internal/e2e/livescan/corpus.go.
id: nx-21.5.0
title: "nx 21.5.0 (npm): s1ngularity postinstall script drives AI coding CLIs and posts stolen tokens to a new GitHub repo"
campaign: s1ngularity-2025
date: "2025-08-26"
kind: npm
ecosystem: npm
name: nx
version: "21.5.0"
benignVersion: "21.4.1"
summary: >-
  On 2025-08-26 an attacker holding a stolen npm publish token pushed eight
  malicious nx releases straight to npm, without provenance: 21.5.0 at 22:32
  UTC, then 20.9.0, 20.10.0, 21.6.0, 20.11.0, 21.7.0, 21.8.0 and 20.12.0 up to
  00:37 UTC, plus malicious releases of seven plugin packages (@nx/devkit,
  @nx/js, @nx/node, @nx/workspace, @nx/eslint, @nx/key, @nx/enterprise-cloud).
  The last clean nx was 21.4.1 (2025-08-22). The token came from a
  pull_request_target workflow that put a PR title into a shell step (merged
  2025-08-21): on 2025-08-24 a PR (the maintainers believe against an outdated
  branch that still had the workflow) made it run publish.yml from the
  attacker's commit, which posted the token to a webhook. In package.json the
  malicious releases changed postinstall from
  `node ./bin/post-install || exit 0` to `node telemetry.js`, a new root-level
  script (the Nx Console VS Code extension also ran it, by installing
  nx@latest). telemetry.js exited on Windows; looked for the claude, gemini and
  q (Amazon Q) CLIs and ran each with a permission-bypass flag
  (--dangerously-skip-permissions, --yolo, --trust-all-tools) and a prompt
  asking it to write the paths of wallet-, key- and secret-like files to
  /tmp/inventory.txt; collected the environment, `gh auth token` (kept if it
  began gho_ or ghp_) and, when `npm whoami` worked, ~/.npmrc; read every file
  the inventory listed; appended `sudo shutdown -h 0` to ~/.bashrc and ~/.zshrc;
  and, given a GitHub token, created a public repository named
  s1ngularity-repository in the victim's account and committed everything to it
  as results.b64 (JSON, base64-encoded three times). Some later releases
  reworded the prompt and suffixed the repository -0 or -1; reports quote
  several wordings, and the one used here is the one Wiz and StepSecurity give
  for 21.5.0. A community issue (nrwl/nx#32522) came at 00:30 UTC,
  StepSecurity's runtime monitoring flagged the install-time api.github.com
  calls, npm removed the versions at 02:44 UTC and GitHub hid the repositories
  at about 09:00 UTC. Wiz counted more than 1,700 users with leaked secrets, and
  the leaked GitHub tokens fed a second phase that made victims' private
  repositories public.
techniques: [ci-compromise, publish-token-theft, install-hook, ai-cli-abuse, credential-theft, github-repo-exfiltration, triple-base64, shell-rc-sabotage]
reconstruction: >-
  telemetry.js follows StepSecurity's full copy of the 21.5.0 script (the diff
  screenshots in the Nx advisory, of 21.7.0, show the same structure and the
  package.json change): a root-level file run by postinstall, the Windows exit,
  the `which` probes for claude, gemini and q with their three permission-bypass
  flags and the "Recursively search local paths" prompt word for word as Wiz and
  StepSecurity quote it for 21.5.0, the gh auth token and ~/.npmrc collection,
  the inventory read-back, and the public s1ngularity-repository that receives
  results.b64 (commit message "Creation."). Changes: the GitHub API host is
  api.github.invalid and the link recorded for the new repository points at
  github.invalid; forceAppendAgentLine() keeps its name, its target list and its
  call site but writes nothing (the original appended `sudo shutdown -h 0` to
  ~/.bashrc and ~/.zshrc). The local steps (CLI probes and runs, token and
  ~/.npmrc reads) are kept as published, so only the upload cannot leave the
  machine. The control leaves out the real 21.4.1's own postinstall
  (`node ./bin/post-install || exit 0`, a local set-up script) and so carries no
  install hook. The rest of the package is a small stand-in for the real one,
  which has 1,352 files.
references:
  - https://github.com/nrwl/nx/security/advisories/GHSA-cxm3-wv7p-598c
  - https://www.stepsecurity.io/blog/supply-chain-security-alert-popular-nx-build-system-package-compromised-with-data-stealing-malware
  - https://www.wiz.io/blog/s1ngularity-supply-chain-attack
  - https://www.wiz.io/blog/s1ngularitys-aftermath
  - https://snyk.io/blog/weaponizing-ai-coding-agents-for-malware-in-the-nx-malicious-package/
legitimateHosts:
  - github.com
  - nx.dev
indicators:
  - node telemetry.js
  - s1ngularity-repository
  - /tmp/inventory.txt
  - "--dangerously-skip-permissions"
-- shared/README.md --
# Nx

The core Nx package: the project graph, the `nx` command and task
orchestration for monorepos.

```sh
npx nx graph
npx nx run-many build
```

Documentation: https://nx.dev
